{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
My [G]grandfather's [D]clock
Was too [G]large for the [C]shelf,
So it [G]stood ninety [D]years on the [G]floor;
It was [G]taller by [D]half
Than the [G]old man him[C]self,
Though it [G]weighed not a [D]pennyweight [G]more.
It was [G]bought on the [Em]morn
Of the [Am]day that he was [D]born,
And was [G]always his [Em]treasure and [A7]pride; [D7]
But it [G]stopped [D7]short, [G]never to [C]go a[G]gain,
When the [G]old [D7]man [G]died.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[G]Ninety years with[D]out [G]slumber[C]ing,
[G]Tick, tock, [D7]tick, tock,
His [G]life seconds [D]number[G]ing, [C]
[G]Tick, tock, [D7]tick, tock,
It [G]stopped [D7]short, [G]never to [C]go a[G]gain,
When the [G]old [D7]man [G]died.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
In [G]watching its [D]pendulum
[G]Swing to and [C]fro,
Many [G]hours had he [D]spent while a [G]boy;
And in [G]childhood and [D]manhood
The [G]clock seemed to [C]know,
And to [G]share both his [D]grief and his [G]joy.
For it [G]struck twenty-[Em]four
When he [Am]entered at the [D]door,
With a [G]blooming and [Em]beautiful [A7]bride; [D7]
But it [G]stopped [D7]short, [G]never to [C]go a[G]gain,
When the [G]old [D7]man [G]died.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
My [G]grandfather [D]said
That of [G]those he could [C]hire,
Not a [G]servant so [D]faithful he [G]found;
For it [G]wasted no [D]time,
And had [G]but one de[C]sire,
At the [G]close of each [D]week to be [G]wound.
And it [G]kept in its [Em]place,
Not a [Am]frown upon its [D]face,
And its [G]hands never [Em]hung by its [A7]side; [D7]
But it [G]stopped [D7]short, [G]never to [C]go a[G]gain,
When the [G]old [D7]man [G]died.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
{x_end: G}
